one: <i>Plane</i> tool, click the corners top-left \u2192 top-right \u2192 bottom-right \u2192 bottom-left, type the real size."}, {"title": "Real speed, not pixel speed", "select": "Robot", "plot": {"q": "speed", "show": ["Robot"]}, "spot": ["plots"], "text": "The robot's <b>speed</b> reads a flat \u2248 0.8 m/s all the way round \u2014 even though on screen it crawls at the far side and races at the near side. That's the perspective correction at work."}, {"title": "Far side vs near side", "seek": 100, "select": "Robot", "spot": ["readout"], "text": "Frame 100: robot at the far side. Compare the <i>At this frame</i> readout here with the next step (near side) \u2014 the speed in m/s is the same."}, {"title": "Near side", "seek": 296, "select": "Robot", "spot": ["readout"], "text": "Frame 296: robot at the near side, moving many more pixels per frame \u2014 same real speed."}, {"title": "What x and y mean here", "plot": {"q": "x", "show": ["Robot"]}, "spot": ["plots", "canvas"], "text": "With a plane calibration, x and y are positions <b>on the floor</b>, in metres: <b>(0, 0)</b> is the taped rectangle's near-left corner, <b>x</b> runs along its 3 m side, <b>y</b> along its 2 m side (away from the camera). The robot loops between about x = \u22120.25\u20263.25 m and y = \u22120.25\u20262.25 m \u2014 and that's exactly the loop it was programmed to drive (checked: within 2.5 cm)."}, {"title": "Distance to the dock", "plot": {"q": "distance", "show": ["Distance to dock"]}, "spot": ["measures", "plots"], "text": "<b>Dock</b> is a fixed point (green square). <i>Distance to dock</i> measures from it to the robot in real metres on every frame."}, {"title": "Try it: move a corner", "seek": 0, "fit": true, "spot": ["canvas"], "text": "Drag one of the yellow corners somewhere else and watch the grid and the speed graph change \u2014 a bad calibration gives wrong numbers. <b>Ctrl+Z</b> undoes it."}], "stab": {}}